  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Properties of triangles, quadrilaterals and regular polygons",
  "summary": "Use angle, side, diagonal and symmetry properties of common shapes.",
  "outcomes": [
    "1.4"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "g1-lesson-03"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "Use angle and side properties of triangles and special quadrilaterals.",
    "Determine line and rotational symmetry of regular polygons and quadrilaterals.",
    "Combine shape properties to solve multi-step angle problems."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "g1-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-14-method",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Properties describe structure",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Triangles have interior-angle sum 180°. Special quadrilaterals are distinguished by parallel sides, equal sides, right angles and diagonal properties. A regular polygon has equal sides and equal angles, with rotational order and number of symmetry lines equal to its number of sides."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "\\text{regular }n\\text{-gon: symmetry order}=n"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-lesson-04-visual",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read the diagram",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Shape properties come from definitions and markings. The diagram shows an isosceles triangle: the tick marks show that two sides are equal, so the two base angles, both marked x, are equal. One equal side is extended beyond the apex to make an exterior angle of 116°. A triangle's angles total 180°, a quadrilateral's total 360°, and a regular polygon has all sides and all interior angles equal."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g1-lesson-04-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-lesson-04-reasoning",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Reasoning method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For a multi-step problem, record every justified property beside the diagram: equal sides, equal angles, parallel sides and symmetry. Then use triangle or quadrilateral angle sums. A diagonal can split a quadrilateral into triangles, but properties of the diagonal depend on the quadrilateral type."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-14-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: an isosceles triangle with an exterior angle",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In the diagram, the exterior angle at the apex of the isosceles triangle is 116°. Find the base angle x."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The exterior angle and the apex angle lie on a straight line, so the apex angle is"
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "180^\\circ-116^\\circ=64^\\circ"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The two base angles are equal, and the three angles total 180°."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "2x+64^\\circ=180^\\circ\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=58^\\circ"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "58° + 58° + 64° = 180°. Also, the exterior angle equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles: 58° + 58° = 116°."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-lesson-04-example-2",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: an isosceles trapezium",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "An isosceles trapezium has two equal angles of 68° on one of its parallel sides. Find the two angles on the other parallel side."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Each pair of angles between the parallel sides, along one slanting side, is co-interior, so they total 180°."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "180^\\circ-68^\\circ=112^\\circ"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The four angles are 68°, 68°, 112° and 112°."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The four angles total 360°, and the angles on each parallel side are equal."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-lesson-04-pitfalls",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors and checks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do not assume a property merely because a drawing looks symmetric. Use stated markings and definitions. A regular polygon requires both equal sides and equal angles. When a diagonal is drawn, identify which new triangles and angle sums it creates."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-14-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "What is the rotational order of a regular hexagon?"
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "6",
        "canonical_latex": "6",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A regular hexagon matches itself six times in one complete turn."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-lesson-04-summary",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Method checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A triangle's angles total 180° and a quadrilateral's total 360°. Equal sides give equal opposite angles in an isosceles triangle, and a regular polygon with n sides has n lines of symmetry and rotational symmetry of order n."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use only the properties that are stated or marked, record them on the diagram, then apply the angle sums."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
